---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import DBox from '../../components/DBox.astro';
import { facts } from '../../data/facts';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '');
---

<Base
  title={`Turning ${facts.booksRead} read books into infrastructure — Andrew Yang`}
  description={`Reading notes turned into a queryable engine: full-text and vector search over roughly ${facts.booksRead} books, served to machines through an authenticated API.`}
>
  <p><a href={`${base}/lab/`}>← Lab</a></p>
  <span class="tag">knowledge systems</span>
  <h1>Turning {facts.booksRead} read books into infrastructure</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    I read a lot, and I keep structured notes — roughly {facts.booksRead} books distilled into
    a <a href={`${base}/platform/`}>{facts.repoCount}-repository platform</a>. That corpus
    is the actual moat: not the tooling around it, but the accumulated judgment
    inside it. The engineering question became: how do you make a decade of
    reading <em>queryable</em> — first for a human, then for machines?
  </p>

  <Diagram
    viewBox="0 0 920 140"
    alt="Three generations left to right: markdown book notes for human re-reading, then one SQLite index with full-text and vector search, then an authenticated API whose main consumer is a news pipeline asking which books resonate with each item. The first arrow is labelled with memory not scaling past a few hundred books; the second with the question changing from search to serving machines."
    caption="The notes stay the single source of truth at every generation — the index is derived and rebuildable, the API is read-only. Only the consumer changed: me, then search, then machines."
  >
    <DBox x={10} y={36} w={230} h={64} title="Gen 1 — notes for humans" sub="one repo per book, chapters" sub2="as markdown, taxonomy" />
    <DBox x={335} y={36} w={230} h={64} title="Gen 2 — one index" sub="SQLite: chapter trees, trigram" sub2="full-text, vector embeddings" />
    <DBox x={660} y={36} w={230} h={64} accent title="Gen 3 — API for machines" sub="authenticated bridge; the news" sub2="pipeline asks what resonates" />
    <path class="flow" d="M240,68 L329,68" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <text class="lbl" x="284" y="26" text-anchor="middle">memory stops</text>
    <text class="lbl" x="284" y="120" text-anchor="middle">scaling</text>
    <path class="flow" d="M565,68 L654,68" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <text class="lbl" x="609" y="26" text-anchor="middle">the consumer</text>
    <text class="lbl" x="609" y="120" text-anchor="middle">stops being me</text>
  </Diagram>

  <h2>Generation 1 — notes for humans</h2>
  <p>
    Hugo book sites, one repository per book, chapters as markdown. Perfect for
    re-reading, useless for asking questions across the collection. "Which books
    say something about this?" meant remembering, which stops scaling around
    book two hundred.
  </p>

  <h2>Generation 2 — an index for search</h2>
  <p>
    Everything distilled into one SQLite file: a chapter tree per book,
    trigram full-text search (chosen because CJK text breaks word-boundary
    tokenizers), and vector embeddings at both book and chapter level. Hybrid
    search with two deliberate modes — <em>find</em> (which books should I
    consult?) ranks books; <em>echo</em> (which passages respond to this
    paragraph?) ranks passages. Different questions, different rankings, one
    index.
  </p>

  <h2>Generation 3 — an API for machines</h2>
  <p>
    A small bridge service puts the index behind an authenticated machine API,
    and the interesting consumer is not a human: the
    <a href={`${base}/lab/async-pipeline-three-generations/`}>news pipeline</a>
    calls it for every incoming item and asks, in effect, <em>"does anything I
    have read resonate with this?"</em> Cosine distance against the corpus is
    the editorial gate — items that echo nothing I know get dropped. My reading
    history literally decides what gets published.
  </p>
  <ul class="clean">
    <li>
      <strong>Distance, not rank, at the gate:</strong> fused ranking scores
      only order results within one query; the go/no-go threshold must use raw
      cosine distance, or the gate's meaning changes with every query. Found by
      testing, recorded as an ADR.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tools wrap the database:</strong> assistants and services go
      through CLI/API wrappers that clean shortcodes and enforce read-only
      access — the raw notes and the index stay authoritative and unwritable
      from the consuming side.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The index is derived, the notes are truth:</strong> the SQLite
      file can be rebuilt from the repositories at any time. One direction of
      derivation, no second source of truth.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why this is the moat</h2>
  <p>
    Any engineer can wire up vector search in an afternoon — the retrieval
    stack is commodity. What cannot be copied is the corpus: {facts.booksRead} books of
    structured, personally-distilled notes, accumulated over years, with a
    taxonomy that makes them navigable. The system's quality ceiling is set by
    the knowledge inside it, and that is exactly the asset that compounds.
  </p>

  <h2>What I'd tell my past self</h2>
  <p>
    Structure the notes as if a machine will read them, years before any
    machine does. The chapter trees, front-matter and taxonomy felt like
    over-engineering for a personal library; they turned out to be the entire
    reason generations 2 and 3 were cheap.
  </p>
</Base>
